---
name: "Showcase — Script & Markup"
markup: true
variables:
  DOCTITLE: ""
  DOCSUBTITLE: ""
  AUTHORNAME: ""
  INTRO: ""
  AUDIENCE: ""
  REVIEWDATE: ""
  PAGES: ""
  PUBLISHED: ""
  MEETINGTIME: ""
types:
  REVIEWDATE: date
  PAGES: number
  PUBLISHED: yesno
  MEETINGTIME: time
---
#SCRIPT
# =====================================================================
#  SCRIPT SHOWCASE  -  every feature of the mini-Python dialect.
#  '#' starts a comment. Blocks are indented with 4 spaces, like Python.
#  Everything assigned below is an OUTPUT: it is filled in for you and is
#  NOT asked as an input when you generate a document.
# =====================================================================

# --- values & assignment --------------------------------------------
name = "mini-latex"        # a str  ("double" or 'single' quotes)
count = 3                  # a number
ready = True               # a bool  (True / False)

# --- string methods -------------------------------------------------
shout   = name.upper()                 # MINI-LATEX
lowered = shout.lower()                # mini-latex
calm    = "  padded  ".strip()         # padded
spaced  = name.replace("-", " ")       # mini latex
zeroed  = str(count).rjust(3, "0")     # 003
dotted  = name.ljust(12, ".")          # mini-latex..
starts  = "yes" if name.startswith("mini") else "no"   # startswith -> yes
ends    = "yes" if name.endswith("x") else "no"        # endswith   -> yes

# --- functions: len / str / float / format --------------------------
name_length = str(len(name))                  # len() -> number -> str()
pi_twice    = str(float("3.14") * 2)          # float() parses text -> 6.28
today       = format(system.date, "MMMM d, yyyy")   # format a date
clock       = format(system.now, "HH:mm")           # format a time
ratio       = format(count / 2, "0.00")             # format a number -> 1.50

# --- slicing & indexing  (on a literal, so it is never empty) -------
first = name[0]        # m
head  = name[:4]       # mini
tail  = name[-5:]      # latex
mid   = name[5:10]     # latex

# --- operators & booleans -------------------------------------------
total     = count * 2 + 1            # 7
leftover  = total % count            # 1
enough    = count >= 3 and ready     # True
has_dash  = "-" in name              # True
missing_x = "x" not in name          # True
plural    = "items" if count != 1 else "item"      # inline choice

# bools rendered as text so they can be printed in the body
enough_txt = "yes" if enough else "no"
dash_txt   = "yes" if has_dash else "no"
x_txt      = "yes" if missing_x else "no"

# --- built-ins: system.* and doc.* ----------------------------------
who       = system.username
machine   = system.machine
platform  = system.os
this_year = str(system.year)
month     = str(system.month)
day       = str(system.day)
at_time   = format(system.time, "HH:mm")
source    = doc.template
created   = format(doc.created, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm")
modified  = format(doc.modified, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm")

# --- if / elif / else -----------------------------------------------
if count >= 5:
    size = "large"
elif count >= 2:
    size = "medium"
else:
    size = "small"

# --- match / case  (AUDIENCE becomes a drop-down when you generate) --
match AUDIENCE:
    case "Beginner":
        tone = "Gentle pace - every step is explained."
    case "Expert":
        tone = "Terse and straight to the point."
    case _:
        tone = "A balanced walkthrough for a general reader."
#ENDSCRIPT
% A line beginning with an unescaped % is a MARKUP comment: it is dropped
% from the output. (Inside the SCRIPT pane above, comments start with #.)

\title{ {DOCTITLE}}
\author{ {AUTHORNAME}}
\date{ {today}}
\maketitle

\begin{center}
\emph{ {DOCSUBTITLE}}
\end{center}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

{INTRO}

This template renders the value of every script variable below. Inline styles: \textbf{bold}, \textit{italic}, \emph{emphasis}, \underline{underline}, \texttt{monospace}, \textsc{small caps}. Symbols: \today, \LaTeX, \TeX, an ellipsis\ldots and a wide\quad gap. Escapes: \% \& \# \$ \_ and a literal backslash \textbackslash. (Braces are written by doubling them, since the template reads a single brace as a placeholder — see the Placeholders section.)

\section{Text styles}

\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Command} & \textbf{Renders as} \\
\textbackslash textbf & \textbf{bold text} \\
\textbackslash textit & \textit{italic text} \\
\textbackslash emph & \emph{emphasised text} \\
\textbackslash underline & \underline{underlined text} \\
\textbackslash texttt & \texttt{typewriter text} \\
\textbackslash textsc & \textsc{small caps} \\
\textbackslash textsl & \textsl{slanted text} \\
\end{tabular}

\section{Lists}

\subsection{Bulleted}

\begin{itemize}
\item First bullet
\item Second bullet, with \textbf{bold} inside
\item Third bullet
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Numbered}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Step one
\item Step two
\item Step three
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Description}

\begin{description}
\item[Script] Runs first and sets the variables.
\item[Markup] Formats the substituted text.
\item[Export] Writes the .docx or .txt.
\end{description}

\section{Script output} \label{sec:script}

\subsection{Values, strings and functions}

\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Expression} & \textbf{Result} \\
name & {name} \\
name.upper() & {shout} \\
shout.lower() & {lowered} \\
"  padded  ".strip() & {calm} \\
name.replace("-", " ") & {spaced} \\
str(count).rjust(3, "0") & {zeroed} \\
name.ljust(12, ".") & {dotted} \\
name.startswith("mini") & {starts} \\
name.endswith("x") & {ends} \\
len(name) & {name_length} \\
float("3.14") * 2 & {pi_twice} \\
format(count / 2, "0.00") & {ratio} \\
\end{tabular}

\subsection{Slicing}

\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Slice} & \textbf{Result} \\
name[0] & {first} \\
name[:4] & {head} \\
name[-5:] & {tail} \\
name[5:10] & {mid} \\
\end{tabular}

\subsection{Numbers, operators and logic}

\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Expression} & \textbf{Result} \\
count * 2 + 1 & {total} \\
total \% count & {leftover} \\
count >= 3 and ready & {enough_txt} \\
"-" in name & {dash_txt} \\
"x" not in name & {x_txt} \\
"items" if count != 1 else "item" & {plural} \\
if / elif / else -> size & {size} \\
\end{tabular}

\subsection{Built-ins and the scenario}

\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Source} & \textbf{Value} \\
system.username & {who} \\
system.machine & {machine} \\
system.os & {platform} \\
system.year & {this_year} \\
system.month & {month} \\
system.day & {day} \\
system.date & {today} \\
system.time & {at_time} \\
system.now & {clock} \\
doc.template & {source} \\
doc.created & {created} \\
doc.modified & {modified} \\
AUDIENCE tone (match) & {tone} \\
\end{tabular}

\section{Inline expressions}

An inline expression — an equals sign and an expression inside braces — is evaluated where it sits and can read any script variable:

\begin{itemize}
\item Two plus three is {= 2 + 3 }.
\item name in capitals: {= name.upper() }.
\item count times one hundred: {= count * 100 }.
\item first four letters: {= name[:4] }.
\item enough? {= "yes" if enough else "no" }.
\end{itemize}

\section{Tables and alignment} \label{sec:tables}

Column specifiers \texttt{l}, \texttt{c}, \texttt{r} set left / centre / right alignment:

\begin{tabular}{lcr}
\textbf{Left} & \textbf{Centre} & \textbf{Right} \\
apples & 3 & 1.50 \\
pears & 12 & 24.00 \\
figs & 1 & 0.75 \\
\end{tabular}

\section{Quote and centre}

\begin{quote}
A block quote is indented as a unit. Good for pull-quotes and notes.
\end{quote}

\begin{center}
This paragraph is centred.
\end{center}

\hrule

\section{Placeholders and literal braces}

A placeholder in braces inserts a variable; a dotted one inserts a built-in. Doubling a brace prints it literally:

\begin{itemize}
\item Document title: {DOCTITLE}
\item Built-in date: {system.date}
\item Built-in doc name: {doc.name}
\item Literal braces: {{not a placeholder}}
\end{itemize}

\section{Typed inputs}

These variables declare a type in the front matter, so the generate window offers the right control (date picker, number, yes/no, time). They stay blank until you fill them in:

\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Field (type)} & \textbf{Value} \\
Review date (date) & {REVIEWDATE} \\
Pages (number) & {PAGES} \\
Published (yes/no) & {PUBLISHED} \\
Meeting time (time) & {MEETINGTIME} \\
\end{tabular}

\newpage

\section{Headings and run-in}

\subsection{A subsection}

\subsubsection{A subsubsection}

\paragraph{Run-in heading.} A \texttt{paragraph} command makes a bold run-in lead-in, and the text continues on the same line.

\section*{Unnumbered section}

A \texttt{section*} heading is not numbered and stays out of the table of contents.

\section{The = formula tier (no-code)}

Separately from the SCRIPT pane, a variable whose \emph{value} begins with \texttt{=} is an Excel-style formula, evaluated once before the script (in dependency order). Type these as a variable's value when generating:

\begin{verbatim}
=2 + 2               a number formula -> 4
=PAGES * 2           references another variable
=upper("hello")      no - methods are script-only; use the SCRIPT pane
'=literal            a leading ' keeps a literal leading =
\end{verbatim}

\section{Literal source (verbatim)}

Verbatim renders text exactly. Braces are doubled so the tokens print literally:

\begin{verbatim}
{{NAME}}                 a variable placeholder
{{system.date}}          a built-in placeholder
{{= 1 + 2 }}             an inline expression -> 3
\section{{Heading}}      a numbered heading
\textbf{{bold}}          inline bold
\begin{{figure}}         a figure (needs a real image file)
\includegraphics{{pic.png}}
\caption{{A caption}}
\end{{figure}}
\listoffigures           lists captioned figures
\end{verbatim}

\section{Cross-references}

\ref refers to a numbered \label. This document's tables are in section \ref{sec:tables}, and the script output is in section \ref{sec:script}.

\hrule

\emph{Rendered by MiniLatex on {= format(system.now, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm") } — audience tone: {tone}}